  <p>
    Block walls are popular across Southern Utah for privacy, sound, and a solid, finished look. They are also the
    project where St. George's rules bite hardest: a retaining wall of the right height can require a city permit,
    and a wall on a slope has to handle water and soil pressure, not just look good. This guide explains the types,
    the permit rule, what drives the price, and how to size up a mason.
  </p>

  <h2>Types of block walls</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Freestanding CMU wall.</strong> Concrete masonry units stacked and mortared into a privacy or boundary wall. Often finished with stucco, paint, or a cap.</li>
    <li><strong>Decorative or split-face block.</strong> The same idea with a textured face that stays exposed. The face texture is the finish, so it needs less coating.</li>
    <li><strong>Segmental (interlocking) block retaining wall.</strong> Dry-stacked units that hold soil, common in landscaping and terraces.</li>
    <li><strong>Reinforced concrete or CMU retaining wall.</strong> Engineered walls with a footing, steel reinforcement, and grout for taller or heavily loaded conditions.</li>
    <li><strong>Rockery.</strong> Large stacked boulders, often used as a natural-looking retaining wall on slopes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The St. George permit rule for walls</h2>
  <p>
    St. George City Code §10-18-5 is the section most homeowners are surprised by. It says that rockery, segmental block,
    reinforced concrete, or CMU retaining walls four feet or greater in height may not be built without first getting a
    permit from the city, unless the wall is shown in detail on an approved site development plan. Separately, it requires
    a permit before you construct any fence or retaining wall in a front setback, again unless shown on approved plans.
  </p>
  <div class="sgfw-permit">
    <strong>How height is measured matters.</strong> For concrete and CMU retaining walls, the city measures from the top
    of the footing to the top of the wall. For rockery and segmental block walls, it measures from the exposed face to
    finished grade next to the base of the wall. A wall that looks like 3 feet above ground can measure differently once the footing is counted, so ask the
    city, not just your contractor.
  </div>
  <p>
    A permit application under §10-18-5 must include a site plan showing property lines, sidewalks, buildings, height,
    and the location of all fences and retaining walls, plus a landscape and drainage plan for retaining walls. The
    <a href="/fence-permit-st-george-ut/">permit guide</a> lays out the steps and where to apply.
  </p>

  <h2>Freestanding walls follow the fence height limits</h2>
  <p>
    A block wall built as a boundary or privacy wall is a fence under §10-18-1, so the same limits generally apply: 4 feet
    in the front yard and 6 feet 4 inches in side and rear yards, with a decorative, at-least-50-percent-open requirement
    within 10 feet behind the sidewalk. A solid block wall would not meet that open requirement, so front-yard walls near a sidewalk are a
    question to settle with the city before you design anything.
  </p>

