  <h2>Sloped lots, drainage, and desert soil</h2>
  <p>
    A retaining wall is holding back soil, and soil pushes. The parts you cannot see are what keep a wall straight:
    a properly prepared base or footing, reinforcement where the design calls for it, and drainage so water does not build up behind the wall. Poor drainage is a
    common reason retaining walls fail. Because §10-18-5 asks for a landscape and drainage plan for retaining walls, the
    city considers drainage part of the project, not an add-on.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ask any company how they handle drainage behind the wall, what is under the first course, and whether the design was
    engineered. On steeper sites or taller walls, ask whether an engineer is involved. Hard or rocky soil can also
    add excavation labor, so a site visit before the quote is a good sign.
  </p>

  <h2>Block versus wood or vinyl</h2>
  <p>
    Block costs more up front than most fences, but it does not rot, does not need painting or sealing on a schedule,
    and blocks noise and sight lines better. Fences are quicker to install and easier to change. If you are weighing the two, compare the total cost of
    ownership rather than the installed price, and check your HOA's rules first because many limit wall materials or heights. Our
    <a href="/fence-installation-st-george-ut/">fence installation guide</a> covers the alternatives.
  </p>

  <h2>Finishes and details that change the quote</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cap style.</strong> A cap course finishes the top and sheds water. Ask what is included.</li>
    <li><strong>Stucco, paint, or veneer.</strong> Coating a plain CMU wall adds material and labor beyond the block itself.</li>
    <li><strong>Pilasters and columns.</strong> Decorative or structural columns add cost.</li>
    <li><strong>Gates and openings.</strong> Cutting in a gate means header details and hardware.</li>
    <li><strong>Demolition of an existing wall or fence</strong> and haul-away.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Planning a block wall, step by step</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Decide the job.</strong> Is it a boundary or privacy wall, or does it hold back soil? That single answer decides which rules and which kind of company apply.</li>
    <li><strong>Measure and mark.</strong> Confirm your property lines, note any slope, and mark where the wall will run, including where it meets existing fences, gates, or the house.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the height rules.</strong> Compare your planned height with the §10-18-1 fence limits for a freestanding wall, or the 4-foot trigger in §10-18-5 for a retaining wall, using the city's method of measuring.</li>
    <li><strong>Get HOA approval,</strong> if you have one, before you order or contract.</li>
    <li><strong>Get two or three written quotes</strong> for the same wall: length, height, block type, footing, reinforcement, drainage, finish, and cleanup.</li>
    <li><strong>Sort out the permit and the plans.</strong> Ask who prepares the site plan and the drainage plan, who submits them, and how long the city review typically takes.</li>
    <li><strong>Have utilities marked, then build.</strong> Footings and posts mean deep digging, so the 811 call comes before any excavation.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Care and maintenance</h2>
  <p>
    Block needs far less upkeep than wood, but not none. Look over the wall each year for new cracks, efflorescence (white mineral
    staining), damaged coatings, and water pooling at the base. Keep irrigation heads from soaking the wall or the soil behind it, and clear weep holes and drains on retaining walls
    so water can leave. A small crack caught early is a small repair.
  </p>

  <h2>Vetting a block wall company</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Verify the company's license status yourself with the <a href="https://dopl.utah.gov/" rel="noopener">Utah Division of Professional Licensing</a>, and confirm insurance directly.</li>
    <li>Ask who pulls the permit and whether the quote includes the site plan and drainage plan §10-18-5 requires for retaining walls.</li>
    <li>Ask for a description of the footing, reinforcement, and drainage in writing.</li>
    <li>Look at completed walls a few years old, not just fresh ones.</li>
    <li>Call 811 (<a href="https://www.bluestakes.org/" rel="noopener">Blue Stakes of Utah</a>) before digging; the service asks for at least 3 business days of notice.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Red flags</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A retaining wall quoted with no mention of drainage or a footing.</li>
    <li>"You don't need a permit" said without measuring the wall or checking your setback.</li>
    <li>A quote that is far below the others with a vague scope.</li>
    <li>No written scope for what is finished, coated, or capped.</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="sgfw-note">
